I am. Okay. Are you in favor of the

02:24.346 --> 02:28.150
consent agenda? Yes. Sorry. That's okay.

02:30.010 --> 02:34.082
Okay, that carries. Okay. So, Councilor Huinink, you wanted the

02:34.186 --> 02:37.250
GRT ridership update report, please.

02:37.290 --> 02:42.034
Thank you. Yes, I noted that because

02:42.122 --> 02:46.278
first undelivered

02:46.294 --> 02:50.198
trips are at an all time low for Mobility plus, which is fantastic.

02:50.294 --> 02:53.974
But I'm concerned that that is in part related to online

02:54.062 --> 02:56.870
booking versus over the phone booking.

02:57.030 --> 03:00.454
Wonder if Commissioner Spooner could speak to that

03:00.542 --> 03:02.290
for a moment, please,

03:03.950 --> 03:07.250
through your chair and Councillor Huinink. Thank you for the question.

03:07.630 --> 03:11.702
I think I'll start by just saying the methodology on this

03:11.726 --> 03:15.010
one has not changed from 2023 to 2024.

03:15.850 --> 03:19.346
We're extremely proud that we brought that underdelivered trip number

03:19.418 --> 03:22.910
from 5.4% down to 2.45%.

03:23.210 --> 03:26.578
Additionally, in the last six months, that's been 1.43%.

03:26.634 --> 03:30.962
So, council, you'll recall you'd given us a target of 2.5% and

03:30.986 --> 03:34.498
very happy to be under that. Councilor Huinink, you pick off a very interesting

03:34.594 --> 03:38.882
challenge, noting that our Mobility plus customers book trips

03:39.026 --> 03:42.898
in two different ways, phone or online, and they do so at about

03:42.954 --> 03:46.018
50, 50 rates. So half book online and half book by phone.

03:46.194 --> 03:49.998
The challenge that we in the count is when somebody calls

03:50.094 --> 03:53.374
that is a single session and we're often able to resolve and find them a

03:53.382 --> 03:56.654
booking in that session when somebody goes online.

03:56.822 --> 04:00.126
If you think about the experience similar to booking a flight,

04:00.318 --> 04:03.982
somebody could go online, look at multiple trips within one session

04:04.046 --> 04:07.198
and then book the one that fits best. And we don't,

04:07.294 --> 04:10.638
we count that as a session. We do not count that as multiple sessions,

04:10.734 --> 04:14.382
despite the fact that that could be somebody either choosing not to take a trip

04:14.446 --> 04:18.450
or actually not finding the trip that fits them. So again, I think

04:18.570 --> 04:22.450
there's a solution here. It likely lies in the software. We're pretender right now

04:22.570 --> 04:25.750
on the update to our Mobility plus replacement.

04:26.170 --> 04:29.922
And so we'll make that a requirement in there that we can accurately capture

04:30.066 --> 04:33.378
sessions. But again, using that

04:33.434 --> 04:37.266
same methodology, we have had a pretty successful year. So I'll pause if there's any

04:37.338 --> 04:41.074
other thoughts. Thank you, Commissioner Spooner. I really do appreciate the work

04:41.162 --> 04:44.588
of you and your team and I'm glad that this, this is noted for future

04:44.644 --> 04:48.588
software just, just because I do think with increasing technology

04:48.724 --> 04:52.172
people are going to be looking more online than over the

04:52.196 --> 04:56.044
phone perhaps, and so want to make sure that we're tracking that effectively.

04:56.172 --> 05:00.524
Thank you so much. Thank you. Thank you Councilor McCabe.

05:00.572 --> 05:04.124
And just really quickly I just wanted to say,

05:04.292 --> 05:07.388
I mean I know this was on consent, but like when there's such good

05:07.444 --> 05:10.828
news like this, it's, I think it's worth just calling it out and

05:10.884 --> 05:13.372
saying, you know, we got to take the wins when we get it right.

05:13.396 --> 05:17.096
And these are some really good numbers. And 10% more service,

05:17.168 --> 05:20.584
8% improvement in on time performance plus the mobility plus

05:20.632 --> 05:24.024
improvements that Doug was just responding to

05:24.032 --> 05:27.304
Councillor Huinink's questions about. And really looking forward

05:27.392 --> 05:31.096
and thank you. I'm glad to see the Conestoga Mall platform improvements

05:31.128 --> 05:34.120
are in there as well. Cause that's a pretty busy hub as well. So just

05:34.160 --> 05:37.576
wanted to say great work to the team. You know, they've responded

05:37.608 --> 05:41.112
to questions and direction from council and particularly

05:41.176 --> 05:44.232
on the Mobility plus piece. So really, really glad to see that.

05:44.256 --> 05:47.562
Thank you. I second all of

05:47.586 --> 05:51.066
that, Councilor McCabe. It's good to see. Okay,

05:51.258 --> 05:55.338
so no other questions from councilors. We will move on to.

05:55.394 --> 05:59.338
And that was just for information. So we will move on to the regular agenda.

05:59.514 --> 06:03.994
We'll start with 7.1, the 2024

06:04.162 --> 06:07.802
Drinking Water Summary report. Commissioner Rose, I will turn

06:07.826 --> 06:11.402
it over to you to introduce this. Thank you, Chair James.

06:11.466 --> 06:15.272
And actually I will be doing the presentation as well. So I'll just

06:15.376 --> 06:18.664
jump right into it. So, in the

06:18.672 --> 06:22.248
region's commitment to reconciliation, we work closely

06:22.264 --> 06:25.512
with first nations to support water as we have a common

06:25.576 --> 06:29.368
goal with them to protect this valuable resource. As a nod

06:29.384 --> 06:32.392
to that, I would like to read the part of the Mohawk version of the

06:32.416 --> 06:36.552
Haudenosaunee Thanksgiving address for water as we

06:36.736 --> 06:40.706
talk about our annual report for

06:40.778 --> 06:44.530
counsel. So the waters. We give thanks to all the waters of the world

06:44.570 --> 06:48.546
for quenching our thirst and providing us with strength. Water is life.

06:48.698 --> 06:51.858
We know its power in many forms. Waterfalls and rain,

06:51.954 --> 06:55.442
mists and streams, rivers and oceans. With one mind

06:55.546 --> 06:59.058
we send greetings and thanks to the spirit of water. Now our minds

06:59.074 --> 07:01.790
are one. Can you go to the next slide please?

07:04.650 --> 07:08.370
The region of Waterloo is the largest municipality in Canada which

07:08.410 --> 07:11.846
relies primarily on groundwater for our water source.

07:12.018 --> 07:16.542
We have the most facilities and infrastructure of any municipality

07:16.606 --> 07:19.998
in Canada. We're reliant on a high degree of

07:20.054 --> 07:23.774
automation with our SCADA system. We supply just shy

07:23.822 --> 07:27.182
of 60 billion liters of water per year.

07:27.286 --> 07:31.230
That's equivalent to 65 Olympic size swimming pools per

07:31.270 --> 07:33.290
day. Next slide, please.

07:34.230 --> 07:37.902
As we move into compliance updates, it's appropriate to start

07:37.926 --> 07:42.070
with an overview of roles. As you may

07:42.110 --> 07:45.686
recall, the Walkerton tragedy happened

07:45.718 --> 07:49.042
in May of 2000 and in 2002,

07:49.178 --> 07:52.646
Justice O'Connor, who led the inquiry

07:52.678 --> 07:56.566
into that tragedy, said that the responsibilities

07:56.758 --> 08:00.406
or sorry. Given that the safety of drinking water is essential for public health,

08:00.478 --> 08:04.662
those who discharge the oversight responsibilities of the municipality should

08:04.686 --> 08:08.980
be held to a statutory standard of care. And the responsibilities

08:09.060 --> 08:12.420
of managing water require caution and prudence by all of us.

08:12.460 --> 08:15.320
Which is a nice segue into the next slide.

08:16.940 --> 08:20.900
So both council as a drinking water system owner and

08:20.940 --> 08:24.132
staff, specifically water and wastewater, who are the

08:24.156 --> 08:28.020
operating authority, have several responsibilities. We must

08:28.060 --> 08:31.700
provide water that meets all prescribed drinking water quality standards.

08:31.860 --> 08:35.046
We have to operate in accordance with the actual the Safe

08:35.078 --> 08:38.310
Drinking Water act and its regulations and keep our system in a good

08:38.350 --> 08:42.470
state of repair. We ensure we are appropriately staffed and

08:42.510 --> 08:46.070
supervised by qualified people, comply with all sampling,

08:46.150 --> 08:50.250
testing and monitoring requirements and meet all reporting requirements.

08:51.310 --> 08:54.822
And you will see in the last slide of this presentation when we get

08:54.846 --> 08:58.278
to it how we were continuing to do this as we prepare for 1 million

08:58.334 --> 09:01.050
people. Next slide.

09:01.970 --> 09:05.882
So this slide is just a reminder for council. It's a snapshot from

09:05.906 --> 09:09.110
the Ministry of the Environment, Conservation and Parks website.

09:09.570 --> 09:13.178
This is a really great guide for members of municipal

09:13.194 --> 09:17.178
councils to understand their individual and collective responsibilities

09:17.274 --> 09:20.794
under the Safe Drinking Water act statutory standard of care.

09:20.962 --> 09:24.410
I find the Guide incredibly useful and

09:24.530 --> 09:29.684
easy to read and understand. So I just wanted to again

09:29.772 --> 09:33.604
as I do yearly, point you to this. We also

09:33.692 --> 09:37.380
have on offer to all members of council who

09:37.420 --> 09:41.444
would like this is not mandatory but highly encouraged to take

09:41.612 --> 09:46.180
a half day course offered by the Walkerton Clean Water center and

09:46.300 --> 09:50.852
Water and Wastewater Services is happy to register

09:50.916 --> 09:54.532
you for that and take care of the funding. If you're interested,

09:54.596 --> 09:58.572
just please send me an email. We want

09:58.596 --> 10:02.044
you to be well informed as the system

10:02.132 --> 10:05.788
owner. Next slide please. So this

10:05.844 --> 10:08.748
talks about our inspections.

10:08.924 --> 10:13.240
So we do get inspected every year by the ministry. We have

10:13.700 --> 10:17.084
19 systems. So essentially the Ministry is here

10:17.172 --> 10:21.500
almost every single day inspecting one of our systems and

10:21.540 --> 10:25.480
I just wanted to talk about the inspections in 2024.

10:26.040 --> 10:29.744
So as I said, they're here rotating

10:29.792 --> 10:33.856
through our systems annually. So we've had 17 system

10:34.008 --> 10:37.808
inspections and we had a 99

10:37.864 --> 10:41.920
inspection rating overall. So 14 of those systems came in at 100%

10:42.040 --> 10:45.680
inspection rating. So that means that we were meeting all of the

10:45.720 --> 10:49.392
requirements of the Safe Drinking Water act where we

10:49.416 --> 10:53.396
did have non compliances, they were related to equipment failures

10:53.568 --> 10:57.148
and we're looking at addressing that obviously through the asset management plan that we'll

10:57.164 --> 11:00.120
be bringing forward later this year.

11:02.100 --> 11:06.040
The region has a very strong compliance track record.

11:07.940 --> 11:10.600
So as I said,

11:11.300 --> 11:15.100
there was five non compliances with equipment. But the region operates

11:15.180 --> 11:19.276
approximately 1500 instruments across 155 water

11:19.348 --> 11:23.494
facilities and we monitor facility performance 24,

11:23.657 --> 11:25.200
7, 365.

11:27.500 --> 11:31.300
So the graph on the right summarizes our compliance score and percentages year

11:31.340 --> 11:35.140
over year. We address non

11:35.180 --> 11:38.596
compliance through taking immediate corrective actions

11:38.628 --> 11:41.876
to fix the issue, completing a root cause analysis

11:41.908 --> 11:45.492
with all involved parties and developing a long term plan to prevent

11:45.556 --> 11:49.690
recurrence of the issue. Slide 6 please.

11:50.150 --> 11:53.198
The other thing that is required is

11:53.254 --> 11:57.054
for the all water systems to have

11:57.062 --> 12:00.622
an internal and external audit. So we are audited twice a

12:00.646 --> 12:03.934
year by an external

12:03.982 --> 12:07.406
company. Even though the internal audit could be done by our staff, we have elected

12:07.438 --> 12:11.010
to bring in someone external. We just think it's more robust.

12:11.910 --> 12:15.370
This slide shows the January 2025 results

12:15.910 --> 12:19.706
and what I'd like to provide to Council are the strengths

12:19.738 --> 12:23.946
that were noted by the auditor. There's a commitment or sorry commitment

12:23.978 --> 12:27.834
to our drinking water quality management system continual

12:27.962 --> 12:31.306
improvement culture. Our management review process

12:31.378 --> 12:34.938
is strong. Our internal audit process is strong and

12:34.994 --> 12:38.538
risk assessment process, infrastructure review process and

12:38.594 --> 12:42.282
operator training tracking is also very strong at

12:42.306 --> 12:45.830
the region. So I just thought I would provide that information to you.

12:46.800 --> 12:50.504
Last slide please. Slide 7. So as I said,

12:50.592 --> 12:54.040
we are planning for the future as we plan for

12:54.080 --> 12:57.736
1 million people, the region's water and wastewater divisions

12:57.768 --> 13:01.272
are focused on four key growth and new

13:01.296 --> 13:05.112
infrastructure. Maintaining existing infrastructure in a state

13:05.136 --> 13:08.380
of good repair. Protecting our water sources,

13:09.280 --> 13:12.922
Operations, maintenance and compliance of our vast network of infrastructure.

13:13.056 --> 13:17.334
It is important to remember that as we grow we will have more infrastructure

13:17.382 --> 13:21.014
to look after to support growth. We are currently working

13:21.102 --> 13:24.390
on the Water Supply Strategy which will create a list of projects and

13:24.430 --> 13:27.590
timing for those projects to support growth and

13:27.630 --> 13:31.014
housing targets in the area municipalities. We are starting

13:31.062 --> 13:34.342
the Wastewater Treatment Master Plan to look for creative and unique

13:34.406 --> 13:38.054
solutions for wastewater treatment given that treated wastewater

13:38.182 --> 13:41.710
in the region is put into the rivers. The 2025

13:41.750 --> 13:44.862
Asset Management Plan will identify the asset renewal and

13:44.886 --> 13:48.750
replacement needs of the $4.3 billion worth of water

13:48.790 --> 13:52.302
and wastewater infrastructure. This includes valves, hydrants,

13:52.366 --> 13:55.650
chambers, pipes, pumps and other important infrastructure.

13:56.230 --> 13:59.550
We are working with the Grand River Source Protection Committee which is

13:59.590 --> 14:03.502
organized by the Grand River Conservation Area on protecting the

14:03.526 --> 14:07.374
water located in the watershed. This is an important initiative

14:07.422 --> 14:11.230
for the environment and also our duty under the Clean Water act and

14:11.270 --> 14:15.066
as part of the MULT barrier approach for safe drinking water as outlined

14:15.098 --> 14:18.842
in the Safe Drinking Water Act. We are also working with

14:18.866 --> 14:23.002
the MECP on a working group which is examining operator license

14:23.066 --> 14:26.378
qualifications. So I'm representing the region

14:26.434 --> 14:29.866
on this group and I've had one on ones

14:29.898 --> 14:33.530
with the region as this is actually an area of expertise for me

14:33.650 --> 14:36.810
in my career. Other operational investments which

14:36.850 --> 14:40.220
Council's made in resourcing have

14:40.260 --> 14:43.868
included through the last Budget 2025 budget have included support

14:43.924 --> 14:48.120
for cybersecurity and this is great because

14:48.420 --> 14:52.236
the MECP has asked in the last couple of years that we include

14:52.308 --> 14:55.836
our approach to security in our quality management systems

14:55.868 --> 14:59.756
for cyber security. That ends my presentation. I'm happy

14:59.788 --> 15:03.240
to take any questions about the report or the drinking water system.

15:05.950 --> 15:09.574
I see no questions. Although Jennifer, I do have a question regarding

15:09.622 --> 15:14.966
some of that asset management and I think Councillor Liggett

15:14.998 --> 15:19.734
asked the Chief this question. In terms of the costs associated

15:19.862 --> 15:22.290
with the asset management,

15:23.710 --> 15:27.510
are we potentially then impacted by some of the tariffs and

15:27.550 --> 15:30.854
that with our asset management, especially in

15:30.862 --> 15:34.262
terms of our water and yeah, can't talk

15:34.286 --> 15:40.290
in the technical terms you would but you know what I'm saying to

15:40.330 --> 15:45.410
the chair, I should say certainly the

15:45.450 --> 15:48.882
valves and replacement of other

15:48.986 --> 15:50.070
appurtenances,

15:52.970 --> 15:56.466
we do keep some on hand so we have some in stock for

15:56.538 --> 15:59.522
emergency repairs or other repairs that we need.

15:59.706 --> 16:04.170
The. The materials for those

16:04.870 --> 16:08.270
repairs could come from the United States and

16:08.310 --> 16:10.130
be impacted by tariffs.

16:11.670 --> 16:15.470
The Asset management plan though will create two

16:15.510 --> 16:19.166
things. One, it will create a list of all of our critical infrastructure

16:19.198 --> 16:23.214
that we need to replace right away

16:23.302 --> 16:26.490
or that we can wait on. And,

16:28.160 --> 16:32.380
and with that we are working with Commissioner Steffler

16:32.960 --> 16:36.744
on an infrastructure funding strategy

16:36.792 --> 16:40.072
with respect to the asset management plan for

16:40.096 --> 16:43.740
all infrastructure of the region, not just water and wastewater infrastructure.

16:45.680 --> 16:49.960
One of the core tenants

16:50.040 --> 16:53.624
of asset management is to extend

16:53.672 --> 16:57.192
the life of the infrastructure as much as possible so

16:57.376 --> 17:01.060
there is opportunity to do repairs until

17:01.180 --> 17:06.052
maybe it is more financially prudent

17:06.116 --> 17:09.684
to do a replacement. So we will be looking at that through the asset management

17:09.772 --> 17:12.920
plan. Okay, thank you. Councilor McCabe,

17:13.980 --> 17:17.780
thank you very much and thanks for the report on this and obviously great

17:17.820 --> 17:21.700
to see that our water system and wastewater system are

17:21.740 --> 17:25.636
in really good, really good positions because obviously

17:25.668 --> 17:29.674
water is as you said, really, really, really critical for everything

17:29.762 --> 17:31.110
that we do in the region.

17:34.450 --> 17:38.234
Thanks for the information about the program that we can take to

17:38.242 --> 17:41.482
just better understand our responsibilities with it. I may

17:41.506 --> 17:45.482
take you up on that. My question with this

17:45.586 --> 17:49.322
is we know we're facing population pressure and

17:49.346 --> 17:53.002
we know we're facing climate change pressures. I'm just wondering if you

17:53.026 --> 17:56.682
could provide some comment on. And we know we don't have. Sorry to like

17:56.786 --> 18:00.450
with the, with the pressures on growth and that. We know we don't have

18:00.830 --> 18:04.134
a lake nearby that we can just. I mean we could build a pipeline,

18:04.182 --> 18:07.782
but I think the estimates are somewhere in the neighborhood of $2 billion if we

18:07.806 --> 18:10.950
were going to have to build a pipeline. So I'm just wondering if you could

18:10.990 --> 18:14.518
comment on some of the potential risks that we

18:14.574 --> 18:18.198
face with where the growth is happening

18:18.294 --> 18:21.810
and the cost to get our infrastructure there. But also

18:22.190 --> 18:25.830
just with climate change, what should we be really or staff really

18:25.870 --> 18:29.516
need to kind of keep an eye on or be concerned

18:29.548 --> 18:32.780
about. Thank you.

18:32.900 --> 18:36.600
Councillor McCabe, through the chair with respect to growth,

18:37.460 --> 18:41.440
we're taking a multi pronged approach with our water supply strategy.

18:42.100 --> 18:45.868
One really is based on conservation, water conservation.

18:45.964 --> 18:49.212
So our team at the region has done a fantastic job of

18:49.236 --> 18:52.800
actually lowering the per capita

18:53.440 --> 18:56.520
amount of water that each person uses in the region.

18:56.640 --> 19:00.024
So that's a big part because that's found capacity

19:00.072 --> 19:03.688
in the system. We're also working outside

19:03.744 --> 19:10.280
of conservation with the area municipalities to understand what

19:10.320 --> 19:14.392
their programs are for leaks in the water pipes themselves

19:14.456 --> 19:17.784
and to see if the region can understand a little bit

19:17.792 --> 19:21.620
more about what the area municipalities are doing because that again is found

19:22.330 --> 19:26.178
easy to find water for water supply.

19:26.274 --> 19:30.130
And these things sound small, but they actually do have a very significant

19:30.250 --> 19:33.270
impact on water supply and water Capacity.

19:34.970 --> 19:39.186
I've also directed staff to look within the region's borders

19:39.298 --> 19:42.738
at a water supply that's available throughout the region.

19:42.874 --> 19:46.882
We do know that there's a lot of water, groundwater in

19:46.906 --> 19:50.860
the region so that we

19:50.900 --> 19:54.220
can support growth that way. So I really do see

19:54.260 --> 19:57.948
this pipeline as something that would be way, way,

19:58.004 --> 20:01.932
way down in the future. Not something we need to consider right now. But I

20:01.956 --> 20:05.516
don't want to impact the REA by making

20:05.588 --> 20:09.148
any suggestions about what the preferred solution is with respect

20:09.204 --> 20:12.492
to water supply in the region. With climate

20:12.556 --> 20:16.186
change, the climate change piece really

20:16.258 --> 20:19.818
is impacting our current and existing assets

20:19.914 --> 20:23.466
more than growth because we need to

20:23.538 --> 20:27.546
make sure that our system does not become

20:27.618 --> 20:30.790
impacted by the big storms that are happening right now.

20:31.090 --> 20:34.426
And the way it does is it's

20:34.458 --> 20:38.154
really on the sewer side, on the sanitary side, so water can

20:38.322 --> 20:42.084
enter our sanitary sewers and take up capacity within

20:42.172 --> 20:45.524
those pipes themselves as well as at the treatment

20:45.572 --> 20:49.140
plants. So when we look at climate change, we're building it into

20:49.180 --> 20:53.252
our asset management plan and we build it into our capital projects all

20:53.276 --> 20:55.640
the time, even the growth projects.

20:56.220 --> 20:59.268
And as I've mentioned before, the Waterloo

20:59.284 --> 21:02.852
Moraine is our source of

21:02.876 --> 21:06.244
water for the region. And through our source protection plans

21:06.292 --> 21:10.308
that we work with the Grand River Conservation Authority and

21:10.364 --> 21:13.776
others in our watershed, we look at protecting

21:13.888 --> 21:16.380
all groundwater sources for drinking water.

21:17.720 --> 21:19.220
Councillor Shantz,

21:21.880 --> 21:25.856
thank you. This is a question that's

21:25.888 --> 21:29.856
sort of down the road and a little bit forward

21:30.008 --> 21:33.696
looking, but I just want to put it out there to get people

21:33.768 --> 21:37.420
starting to think about it. I met with

21:37.840 --> 21:42.040
someone who was recommending using ground,

21:42.160 --> 21:46.500
not groundwater, but water

21:46.800 --> 21:50.140
through infrastructure for heating.

21:50.880 --> 21:54.536
And I wondered if you're

21:54.568 --> 21:58.712
aware of those kind of projects. And you

21:58.736 --> 22:02.296
know, is that something that we can look to in the future

22:02.368 --> 22:05.578
at some point? And the other piece would be,

22:05.634 --> 22:09.850
and this would also be a really big undertaking,

22:09.930 --> 22:14.634
would be using some gray

22:14.682 --> 22:18.538
water, or maybe not. So I'm

22:18.554 --> 22:22.154
thinking in Elmira particularly, right, we pump

22:22.202 --> 22:25.562
the water. It is actually drinking water clean when it

22:25.586 --> 22:29.082
goes into the creek. But I

22:29.106 --> 22:32.478
think there would be a reluctance yet to use it at this

22:32.534 --> 22:35.790
point for, for drinking water. But there are lots of other things

22:35.830 --> 22:39.150
it could be used for. So I just put that

22:39.190 --> 22:42.510
out there as, as future thoughts and wonder if you've given any

22:42.550 --> 22:46.302
thought to any of those to the

22:46.326 --> 22:50.206
councilor through the chair. Those are, those are wonderful and amazing thoughts and absolutely,

22:50.238 --> 22:54.078
we've been having those thoughts as well. We do have a gray water

22:54.134 --> 22:58.274
pilot actually. That's, that is happening right now with some homes

22:58.462 --> 23:02.426
within the region. To see how people use gray

23:02.458 --> 23:06.346
water. And for those who don't know what that is, that's untreated water

23:06.418 --> 23:09.990
that people can use to flush their toilets, for example, or for

23:10.370 --> 23:11.950
using in their gardens.

23:13.570 --> 23:17.354
We are looking at gray water as part of our water supply strategy

23:17.402 --> 23:21.190
under conservation. And we're also looking at it

23:22.530 --> 23:25.722
for other ways, other reasons as well. So for instance,

23:25.786 --> 23:29.950
in wastewater treatment plants or wastewater collection systems,

23:31.950 --> 23:35.190
you can use treated wastewater to clean the

23:35.230 --> 23:39.030
pipes that are within the treatment plant campus

23:39.110 --> 23:42.518
and within the collection system. This is

23:42.574 --> 23:46.182
really exciting because as you say, we don't have to use treated

23:46.246 --> 23:49.606
potable water to clean those systems. And that water just

23:49.678 --> 23:53.894
goes back to the treatment plant. Those are other conservation

23:53.942 --> 23:58.006
methods that we can look at within the region and with partnership

23:58.038 --> 24:01.458
with our area municipalities. Using water

24:01.514 --> 24:04.990
for heating, that is actually something that I am involved in.

24:05.370 --> 24:08.270
It's called district or thermal energy.

24:08.730 --> 24:12.210
And I'm on a working group that's looking at a

24:12.250 --> 24:16.082
region wide. Looking on, sorry, a feasibility study for

24:16.106 --> 24:20.146
a region wide thermal energy idea

24:20.178 --> 24:24.098
to see whether this could even work. And that's where water is

24:24.154 --> 24:28.390
pulled from the ground, heated and used to heat homes and businesses.

24:28.780 --> 24:32.324
You may have heard of the one in Toronto, N Wave,

24:32.372 --> 24:36.100
I believe it's called that does this and cools also, they use water

24:36.140 --> 24:40.132
for cooling purposes in the summer. Those are things that

24:40.236 --> 24:43.572
we're already working on. The District energy one is

24:43.596 --> 24:46.400
not as far advanced as using greywater though.

24:50.700 --> 24:54.532
Okay, thank you. It reminds me of the Activa homes, that development

24:54.636 --> 24:58.372
that they have where there's a pilot project with the gray water. And so

24:58.396 --> 25:02.416
there's, I think, lots of opportunities going forward.

25:02.568 --> 25:05.792
Okay, I need a mover and a seconder for this. Councilor Foxton,

25:05.856 --> 25:09.440
Councillor McCabe, you will be prompted in e scribe. Thank you,

25:09.480 --> 25:12.460
Jennifer, for this report and the work your team is doing.

25:35.490 --> 25:39.870
Councilor Wolf, Councilor Shantz, Councilor Liggett.

25:43.490 --> 25:46.714
Yeah, it's not coming up for me. I'm in favor.

25:46.842 --> 25:50.298
Okay, so that is carried.

25:50.394 --> 25:54.746
Okay, we are Moving on to 7.1.2.

25:54.898 --> 25:59.046
This is the authorization to expropriate land, Lancaster Street

25:59.118 --> 26:03.366
West. I will call on solicitor

26:03.478 --> 26:05.490
Fiona McCray for this.

26:10.190 --> 26:13.670
Thank you. And through you, Chair James, this report

26:13.790 --> 26:17.126
is our typical first report

26:17.198 --> 26:20.890
under the Expropriations Act. And this is an authorization

26:21.230 --> 26:24.170
to make application for expropriation.

26:24.570 --> 26:28.322
And in order to meet the timelines for the project, it's necessary

26:28.386 --> 26:31.190
that expropriation proceedings begin.

26:31.690 --> 26:35.362
However, the region's agents are working with the property

26:35.426 --> 26:39.234
owners to try and come to a

26:39.242 --> 26:42.402
mutual agreement regarding the acquisition of the lands without the need for

26:42.426 --> 26:46.242
expropriation. But as I said, in order to make

26:46.266 --> 26:50.190
sure that we have possession for the timelines, it's necessary to start the process

26:50.540 --> 26:53.812
and it will be discontinued once all the

26:53.836 --> 26:57.300
lands are acquired. Okay,

26:57.460 --> 27:00.600
thank you, Councilor Vrbanovic.

27:03.340 --> 27:06.772
Thank you very much. Chair James Just

27:06.876 --> 27:10.000
on that, in terms of a timeline issue,

27:10.940 --> 27:14.708
I'm always reluctant to go this

27:14.764 --> 27:18.616
route if in fact we're

27:18.648 --> 27:22.136
negotiating still and in negotiations haven't broken

27:22.168 --> 27:26.152
down because I think it's much more

27:26.176 --> 27:30.072
appropriate to be working,

27:30.176 --> 27:33.784
to negotiate a freestanding agreement with

27:33.872 --> 27:38.700
people. What is the absolute deadline

27:39.440 --> 27:43.346
for when we would need to do this to accommodate

27:43.378 --> 27:50.978
the work through

27:51.034 --> 27:55.150
you? Chair James in terms of the expropriation process,

27:55.450 --> 27:58.930
we time these reports so that the

27:58.970 --> 28:02.226
process can be completed to meet the timeline. I don't,

28:02.258 --> 28:06.098
I'll have to refer to Commissioner Rose in terms of the

28:06.234 --> 28:09.486
construction timeline for the project. But we,

28:09.558 --> 28:13.470
we don't initiate the proceeding so far in advance

28:13.550 --> 28:16.010
that there's a lot of extra time.

28:16.310 --> 28:19.726
So this is typically the time when

28:19.798 --> 28:23.518
in the project timeline where we would do this. And yeah,

28:23.534 --> 28:27.406
I mean, I, I guess when I look at the, the, the timeline

28:27.438 --> 28:28.410
of the project,

28:30.310 --> 28:34.346
there's some initial money available in 25,

28:34.518 --> 28:37.922
but the majority of the money shows this is a project that

28:37.946 --> 28:40.830
goes from 26 to 29.

28:41.450 --> 28:45.522
And, you know, I'm, I'm questioning why we couldn't

28:45.586 --> 28:49.150
continue to negotiate with, with people

28:49.690 --> 28:53.282
using a conventional process. Because, because, I mean,

28:53.306 --> 28:57.650
expropriation is, is a very strong

28:57.770 --> 29:01.868
tool that municipalities had and it should only be used

29:02.004 --> 29:05.644
in, you know, when it's absolutely

29:05.692 --> 29:09.372
necessary. So through, through the

29:09.396 --> 29:13.372
chair, to Councillor Vrbanovic, the, the way the timing

29:13.436 --> 29:17.320
works is we do land acquisition in order to,

29:18.420 --> 29:21.900
to relocate utilities. So whether

29:21.940 --> 29:24.956
they're, whatever utilities happen to be under the,

29:25.028 --> 29:28.060
under the road or the sidewalk to make room for the new

29:28.100 --> 29:31.240
infrastructure. And that usually happens. Right?

29:32.240 --> 29:35.528
You'll actually see physical construction on the road, and that's

29:35.544 --> 29:39.704
why you'll, you see the costs starting in 2026

29:39.872 --> 29:43.460
for construction. So that's typically the way that we,

29:43.760 --> 29:47.800
we run our construction projects is property acquisition,

29:47.880 --> 29:51.900
utility relocates, and then actual physical construction

29:52.720 --> 29:54.500
you see in the next year.

29:55.520 --> 29:58.824
No, and, and, and I appreciate that. Thank you, Commissioner Rose,

29:58.872 --> 30:02.136
for that explanation. But again,

30:02.288 --> 30:05.666
I'm, I'm, I'm not clear on

30:05.738 --> 30:10.082
why in March, on March 4th of 2025,

30:10.266 --> 30:13.346
for work that's going to start in 2027,

30:13.498 --> 30:19.778
we need to take the action of expropriation when

30:19.914 --> 30:23.442
in fact, we, you know, could still be

30:23.466 --> 30:27.490
in a situation of freely negotiating it and still

30:27.530 --> 30:29.390
have that tool available to us.

30:30.760 --> 30:34.208
It's a much better way to go. Fiona,

30:34.224 --> 30:37.904
I think, is going to respond. Thank you, Chair James.

30:37.952 --> 30:41.740
And through you, this is just the first step of the process.

30:42.360 --> 30:45.872
Negotiations will be continuing throughout the process with the

30:45.896 --> 30:50.000
owners that have already begun. So this in no way impairs our

30:50.040 --> 30:53.744
ability to continue to work with the owners to reach a mutual

30:53.792 --> 30:58.552
agreement. And that's what we always endeavor

30:58.616 --> 31:02.152
to do. But the whole

31:02.216 --> 31:05.620
process for expropriation takes nine to 12 months

31:06.000 --> 31:09.272
because there are various procedural steps that have to go on and

31:09.296 --> 31:13.192
they have statutory timelines associated with them. So if we

31:13.216 --> 31:17.000
want to ensure that possession of the land is in hand when

31:17.040 --> 31:20.904
the work needs to begin, that's why the process has started

31:20.992 --> 31:24.840
now. And this is consistent with most

31:24.880 --> 31:28.524
of the roads projects that the region has done over

31:28.592 --> 31:30.400
the last 10 plus years.

31:32.620 --> 31:36.820
Okay, I guess I would

31:36.860 --> 31:40.276
like to move a referral of this to

31:40.348 --> 31:44.760
council and ask in the meantime that we see a project timeline

31:45.260 --> 31:49.172
that shows the different phases

31:49.236 --> 31:52.740
of the project and how this fits into

31:52.780 --> 31:56.338
it so that we can make a, you know,

31:56.394 --> 31:59.666
a fully informed decision before we,

31:59.818 --> 32:03.010
before we proceed with this. So, Madame Chair,

32:03.050 --> 32:06.450
at the appropriate time, I would like to move a referral for that

32:06.490 --> 32:10.530
purpose. Okay, so you're the, you're the last speaker, Council Member

32:10.570 --> 32:13.630
Vrbanovic. So just clarifying, it would be until the,

32:14.010 --> 32:16.030
the March Council meeting.

32:17.050 --> 32:21.042
That's correct. And that staff would provide that additional information in

32:21.066 --> 32:25.290
a, in a report to us for that meeting. And I'll second that.

32:26.390 --> 32:29.774
Okay, I. Okay, so Councillor Liggett, you seconded

32:29.822 --> 32:33.182
that? Any speakers to that

32:33.286 --> 32:34.290
referral?

32:38.150 --> 32:40.862
No. Okay, then we can vote. Thank you. Oh,

32:41.006 --> 32:45.050
Councillor, go ahead. Yeah, I'm not quite sure what other information

32:45.670 --> 32:48.330
staff could bring back that we don't have today.

32:52.320 --> 32:56.600
Councilor Vrbanovic, do you just want to clarify what specific information

32:56.720 --> 33:00.872
you want staff to bring back for, for us and staff?

33:01.056 --> 33:05.320
Yeah, so what I'm looking for is a, a project management

33:05.440 --> 33:09.660
timeline for the different phases of the project that shows

33:10.560 --> 33:14.184
land acquisition and, and all the other phases

33:14.312 --> 33:17.514
and why expropriation needs to take place now

33:17.672 --> 33:21.110
versus, you know, why it can't be considered a little

33:21.150 --> 33:25.318
later on while negotiation continues in a non expropriation

33:25.414 --> 33:28.486
manner and when on a referral.

33:28.598 --> 33:32.470
Chair James, I think the only thing we're allowed to actually debate is

33:32.510 --> 33:34.050
timing, just to be clear.

33:38.030 --> 33:41.590
Okay, so. Okay, so then

33:41.630 --> 33:45.462
the timing that's proposed is, is the next council meeting when this would

33:45.486 --> 33:48.596
come back anyway. So just looking for that Additional information.

33:48.758 --> 33:51.960
Okay, it's the next council meeting. I just want to clarify. We're not expropriating

33:52.040 --> 33:54.968
anything at this point in time like that is not happening.

33:55.064 --> 33:58.120
There's still, as Fiona said, there are still discussions

33:58.200 --> 34:01.880
going on about that. But an expropriation process does

34:01.920 --> 34:05.576
take, what did you say, eight to nine months or something like that. So we're

34:05.608 --> 34:09.112
just declaring. So we're not at the expropriation time. So getting a

34:09.136 --> 34:12.248
timeline for the project is one thing, but I think I've

34:12.264 --> 34:15.682
heard that you think there's an exprop rating now, which is not the

34:15.706 --> 34:19.746
case. To, to be clear, this starts the

34:19.818 --> 34:23.870
expropriation process and which,

34:24.410 --> 34:28.050
you know, means that we are

34:28.170 --> 34:33.150
enacting a process that does not see

34:33.770 --> 34:37.090
a non. The threat of expropriation be

34:37.130 --> 34:40.866
part of an. Of a negotia, a free negotiation with the property

34:40.898 --> 34:43.680
owners. And so it is,

34:44.060 --> 34:47.780
I believe, appropriate to understand

34:47.900 --> 34:51.572
why that's necessary versus why the

34:51.596 --> 34:55.300
negotiations can't continue for a period of time before

34:55.340 --> 34:58.404
we consider that. Okay,

34:58.452 --> 35:01.600
Councillor McCabe, thank you.

35:01.900 --> 35:05.604
I just want to make sure I understand this is this, this is the standard

35:05.652 --> 35:09.132
process we use for other projects like

35:09.156 --> 35:12.460
this. And I'm also wondering if

35:12.500 --> 35:16.444
we, if we sort of took another process,

35:16.532 --> 35:19.932
would we set a precedence for how

35:20.036 --> 35:23.520
other projects may or may not be able to proceed?

35:26.020 --> 35:29.532
Is that something we should be concerned with? I just want

35:29.556 --> 35:33.468
to make sure we're covering our bases. Maybe I could offer some

35:33.524 --> 35:37.240
clarification. So this is the

35:37.280 --> 35:40.840
first step of property acquisition.

35:41.000 --> 35:44.568
I understand what Councillor Vrbanovic is saying. However,

35:44.624 --> 35:48.200
we have already started negotiations with the

35:48.240 --> 35:52.328
property owners. This is the first step

35:52.384 --> 35:56.200
to start expropriation. If we have to go that route,

35:56.360 --> 36:00.008
and that is not our intent. Our intent is to

36:00.144 --> 36:03.582
