36:03.776 --> 36:07.730
When we do do that, their property is taken off the

36:07.770 --> 36:11.030
list for expropriation.

36:12.010 --> 36:15.522
And if we can't get to a settlement, then I believe we

36:15.546 --> 36:18.786
go to the second. I would ask our regional

36:18.818 --> 36:22.402
solicitor to speak on that process because

36:22.426 --> 36:25.810
I'm not quite sure, but usually this takes. Because we do

36:25.850 --> 36:30.110
do negotiation, it takes 18 months as opposed to

36:31.170 --> 36:34.618
a 12 month, 9 to 12 month time period

36:34.674 --> 36:38.250
if it was just pure expropriation. So we do build in

36:38.370 --> 36:41.770
the extra time for negotiations. I hope that

36:41.810 --> 36:42.750
clarifies.

36:44.930 --> 36:48.550
Yes. Okay. Councillor Shantz.

36:53.010 --> 36:53.910
Thank you.

36:57.200 --> 37:00.392
Is this wording any different from

37:00.496 --> 37:03.672
what we've seen before? Like, I remember when

37:03.696 --> 37:07.544
I. 10 years ago, when I first started, I thought it was really odd

37:07.592 --> 37:11.144
to have emotion to expropriate

37:11.272 --> 37:13.660
when we were really negotiating.

37:14.720 --> 37:17.832
And I understand that causes some concern and

37:17.856 --> 37:21.576
confusion in the community as well. But I'm just wondering,

37:21.608 --> 37:26.674
is this different from any other property

37:26.722 --> 37:29.906
acquisition motion? Is there something in

37:30.058 --> 37:33.538
different? Because my understanding through this time

37:33.594 --> 37:38.066
was that we just put that on the table as a possibility

37:38.178 --> 37:43.250
of all negotiations fail through

37:43.290 --> 37:46.482
you, Chair James. This is our standard process and

37:46.506 --> 37:50.050
it's the same wording and format that we use for our first

37:50.090 --> 37:53.010
and second reports through the process.

37:53.470 --> 37:56.726
I believe negotiations with the affected owners

37:56.758 --> 38:01.782
on this project have been underway since last summer and

38:01.886 --> 38:05.910
progress is being made and they will continue as

38:05.950 --> 38:09.494
per the normal process. But we are following the typical process

38:09.582 --> 38:13.462
that we have for, I think at least 10 years in

38:13.486 --> 38:15.890
terms of the Roads Capital program.

38:17.080 --> 38:20.640
Thank you, Councilor Williams.

38:20.720 --> 38:23.872
Councilor Vrbanovic I'll come back to you just because you haven't heard from

38:23.896 --> 38:26.992
Councilor Williams yet. Councillor Williams, go ahead.

38:27.176 --> 38:30.368
Oh, thank you. Yeah, I just. I had a quick question of clarification.

38:30.464 --> 38:34.240
So this is just like the standard process as I understand it so

38:34.280 --> 38:38.160
far. But if we didn't do this and get it in in time,

38:38.280 --> 38:42.260
would it delay the project overall, like for the final timeline

38:46.290 --> 38:50.074
through you, Chair James, Depending on which property

38:50.202 --> 38:53.850
we didn't have in time, it may cause a delay in the project

38:53.970 --> 38:57.274
or the scheduling of the work. The phasing of

38:57.282 --> 39:01.146
the work may have to have to change to accommodate that

39:01.218 --> 39:05.034
late timeline. It really depends on the specific property and

39:05.122 --> 39:06.950
how it's impacted by the project.

39:07.980 --> 39:10.680
Okay, thank you, Councilor Liggett.

39:15.980 --> 39:19.572
Perhaps it's in the wording of the motion because the motion says to start the

39:19.596 --> 39:21.760
process for expropriation. Right.

39:40.150 --> 39:43.050
Can you put up the original motion on the screen, please?

40:24.080 --> 40:27.508
Item number one, it says for approval to expropriate land.

40:27.584 --> 40:28.200
There.

40:51.700 --> 40:55.400
Okay, Fiona, go ahead. Thank you, Chair James. And through you,

40:56.090 --> 41:01.842
the introductory paragraph does reference

41:01.906 --> 41:05.122
expropriation of the lands for the project, but the action to

41:05.146 --> 41:08.930
be taken is to complete the application to the council

41:09.050 --> 41:12.658
of the region of the Waterloo as required for

41:12.714 --> 41:16.210
approval to expropriate land. So there's two

41:16.250 --> 41:19.698
reports involved in this process. So there's

41:19.714 --> 41:22.996
the original application report following this.

41:23.068 --> 41:26.532
Then there's notice of the application that's circulated to all

41:26.556 --> 41:30.132
the owners and all the parties that have interest in the

41:30.156 --> 41:32.680
property. And there's notice published in the paper.

41:33.180 --> 41:36.724
There's an opportunity for an owner to request an inquiry,

41:36.772 --> 41:40.196
or what's commonly known as a hearing in necessity, if they have concerns

41:40.228 --> 41:44.292
about the project and whether or not the lands are reasonably required for

41:44.316 --> 41:47.812
implementation of the Project that process in

41:47.836 --> 41:51.518
and of itself, if there is one, can take about six, six to nine months,

41:51.574 --> 41:55.250
depending on availability of an inquiry officer.

41:55.830 --> 41:59.486
And then there's the second report. So earlier in council's agenda,

41:59.518 --> 42:02.654
there was a second report for the Shipley Bridge project.

42:02.822 --> 42:06.222
And that's the report where the actual expropriation

42:06.286 --> 42:09.630
is approved. And then within three months of that

42:09.670 --> 42:13.374
decision, the expropriation plan is to be registered.

42:13.422 --> 42:16.850
And that's the action that expropriates

42:17.160 --> 42:20.208
transfers title to the region through the expropriation

42:20.304 --> 42:23.552
process. So this is initiating the process.

42:23.656 --> 42:27.216
If we end up having negotiated

42:27.248 --> 42:31.180
